A||N||15||Justin||Found an 86 GT, is it a good deal?||||1||
Z||000000||Justin||07-26-2001||10:24 AM||||I am 16 and am looking for my first car, and I have been looking at GT's.  I found an 86 GT that seems like a good deal.  Here is the ad:<P>86 Pontiac Fiero GT, V6, 4 spd., AC, Sunroof, Alloys, PW, Cruise, Warranty. Avail..............Special Cash Price $1,999<P>It talked to the person over the phone, they said the clutch was just replaced, and it has 128,000 miles.  It is red with silver striping at the bottom.  It looks from the picture that it is in good condition.  What do you guys think?  Is it a good deal?  I don't want to spend more then $3,000, so this is perfect.  Anything I should look for when test driving it and checking it out?  I'm really not looking for a project car, so I don't want problems to deal with.<P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<P>visit <A HREF="http://www.twinkiesproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twinkiesproject.com</A>  for information on the chemical properties, reactions, gravitational resonse, and density issues of a Twinkie.||||reg||1
Z||000001||jake1852||07-26-2001||10:40 AM||||there is a sh*tload of stuff to watch for, but you could always just do the usual - check the oil before it is started, look for sh*t in the oil. start it, look for smoke out the exhaust, rev the rpms and watch the temp guage and the oil pressure guage, and anything i forgot. <P>dont give the guy what he wants for it! offer him a bunch less and haggle. <BR>if you dont want it, just kick the tires, and say, "if you give me 50 bucks i will haul this piece of trash off of your property"<BR>if the guy knows nothing, he might just go for it!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Justin||07-26-2001||10:53 AM||||LOL Jake the first half was useful, but It's coming from a used car lot so if I kick the tire the only thing that's gonna get hauled off the propert is me!!!  Yeah I ws thinkin if it's in good shape maybe asking $1,600 for it.  Maybe he will take that.  But this is only if it's in good shape.  If not , maybe I'll offer him less.  Or if it's in mint condition (highly unlikely) I'll just accept the $1,999.  Anyway thanks for the input.<P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<P>visit <A HREF="http://www.twinkiesproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twinkiesproject.com</A>  for information on the chemical properties, reactions, gravitational resonse, and density issues of a Twinkie.||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||07-26-2001||11:10 AM||||Hey Justin, If you like I will go look at it for you or with you.  Email me if you would like to set up something.  Just saw a 88 Formula in Lancaster for $850.  That would have the V6, it needs interior pieces which would be fairly easy to get except the drivers seat.  I didn't hear it run though. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||07-26-2001||11:10 AM||||Check the things mentioned before by Jake1852.<BR>Also open the front and turn on the A/C, the fan should come on. If it doesn't there is something wrong with the fan circuit, don't forget to see it it cools!<BR>Look closely underneath for rust in the area of the floor pan around the plugs, don't be afraid to poke at them! (take a screwdriver with you)<BR>In the trunk pull back the carpeting on both sides looking for rust as well, don't forget to probe with screwdriver.<BR>Headlights for operation, all gauges, all dash light indicators, e-brake for function and condition of tire tread.<BR>While doing the test drive look for vibrations (rolling), sound of engine and proper oil pressure and temp. when hot.<BR>Obvious defects in condition of paint like cracks on the front and rear fascias.<BR>Hope this helps! And welcome to the Forum!<P>Use Skitime's expertise, it's a great offer!<BR>Look at the formula!<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 07-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Justin||07-26-2001||11:50 AM||||Skitime:<BR>My Dad is probably going to go with me and he is good at detecting bad things in cars, but he just wanted to ask you guys what types of things are commonly bad on these cars so he knows more of what to look for.  He probably won't have any trouble telling me if it's bad or not.  As for the formula, no offense, but I think they are ugly.  They ar the notch back design AND have the ugly nose.  This of course is just my opinion, and I am very picky   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  If I get a Feiro it will be a 86 to 88 GT, the 85's have the notchback rear end.  Geez I am really picky.  Anyway I might go look at it this weekend, I'll let you know how it goes.  <P>BTW:  I don't use this board much, but when I do I have to type in my password each time.  I cannot remember an odd collection of random letters so is there an option on here to save your password?  <P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<P>visit <A HREF="http://www.twinkiesproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twinkiesproject.com</A>  for information on the chemical properties, reactions, gravitational resonse, and density issues of a Twinkie.<p>[This message has been edited by Justin (edited 07-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||07-26-2001||12:25 PM||||Great, Have your dad check it out if you have any questions call or email. As you know I prefer the GTs too so there is nothing wrong with going with what you like.  You should start spending more time here to learn as much as you can about the Fieros.  <P>We missed you at the Fiero picnic last weekend.  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/008980.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/008980.html</A>  Did you get my email about it? <P> If you go to the top of the page and click on "profile" you can change your password to something easy to remember.  <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 07-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Justin||07-26-2001||01:17 PM||||Thanks now I can remember my password  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Yes I got your email but my service is through Excite and apparantly nobody has been getting emails from me, there service is completely messed up.  I will have to find another email account.  Any suggestions?  I was busy that day anyway, I went to check out a 1987 Camaro IROC Z28, it had a V8 with a five speed, we test drove it and everything, very cool car.  The guy wanted $3500 for it but insurance was going to be almost $1,400 a year!!!  I don't have that kind of money, so I will get a cheap GT and ust get minimal coverage, should be around $500 a year.  Not to bad if ya ask me.  Well maybe the next time we have a picinic I will drop in as a suprise with my new Fiero?  Well, hopefully my new Fiero that is.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<P>visit <A HREF="http://www.twinkiesproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twinkiesproject.com</A>  for information on the chemical properties, reactions, gravitational resonse, and density issues of a Twinkie.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||07-26-2001||01:37 PM||||I think it would be very rare to get a good car, or a good deal from a used car lot.<P> before you put any cash down get the VIN number and run it through CARFAX. I saw a GT at a dealer in PA and it had a junked or wrecked title, or whatever you call it - the car had been totalled and rebuilt - I dont remember where it was in PA, but there is a good chance this is the same car.<P> if the dealer is selling it for $2k then he got it at an auction or from the last owner for about $1k or so - thats how they make money - most dont recondition cars, they buy low and sell high. If he can buy it for $1k with a little leg work you can get one like it for $1k too.<P>  to get a older car, like a Fiero, in really great condition, or a great deal on one, you have to find one that was well taken care or, rebuilt, or left in a garage for a long time, and buy it from the owner - this is only my personal opinion but I would buy an older car from a used car lot only as a LAST resort.||||reg||11
Z||000009||skitime||07-26-2001||02:18 PM||||Hey Justin, The local Fiero gang are going to Maple Grove Dragway on Sunday for an all Pontiac Show similar to the one you went along to in Mechanicsburg except some will be drag racing their Fieros.  You should go along. You might find out about a Fiero for sale there.   <A HREF="http://www.maplegroveraceway.com/events/pontiacs.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.maplegroveraceway.com/events/pontiacs.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||GodSend||07-26-2001||04:39 PM||||make sure that the guy has not removed the check engine light (its easy to check, 3 torx screws).  If he has removed this to hide problems chances are he will not be honest with anything else either...  Good Luck.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||stsmithgt||07-26-2001||07:13 PM||||Listen for that &^$#%^%()* exhaust leak that is usually present on a car that's "for sale".  See if the:<BR>* emergency brake cable is functioning correctly since is does adjust your rear brakes.  <BR>* the cruise controll work?<BR>* sunroof seal up?<BR>* are the door hinges worn out?<BR>* rattle in the cat?<BR>* alignment?<BR>* rust in the trunk or battery tray?<BR>* wind deflector for the sunroof around?<BR>* scratched windows?  Do they go up and down?<BR>* All lights including sidemarker and "PONTIAC" in the back lite up?<BR>* headlights go up and down?<BR>* sub-woofer work?<BR>* leaking axle seals?<BR>* hub or suspension noise?<BR>* worn out shocks and struts?<BR>* Ummm Ummm what else?????¿¿¿ <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||stimpy||07-26-2001||07:37 PM||||I'd be casting a very skeptical eye at a Fiero GT that a dealership is asking $2000 for.  If you get a bad vibe from the car, walk away. If you have $3000 cash to spend, that should buy a well preserved 86 GT from a private party. I paid $2500 for an 86 with 125k miles on it, but it was very solid and well preserved. If this car that you a looking at is clean and tight, buy it quick.||||reg||1
Z||000013||87GTZ34||07-27-2001||12:03 AM||||In addition to sts' list - <BR>- check for the usual oil leaks front (harmonic balancer), rear main and valve covers.<BR>- try to wiggle the water pump pulley and see how much play there is.<BR>- check the coolant tubes that run along each side to make sure they aren't crushed.<BR>- make sure there is a thermostat in it and get the car hot to check for coolant leaks. Also, the overflow tank (in the front)should have coolant in it (not sucking air). If it does, the car most likely has a coolant leak.<BR>- with the car off, and the key positioned so you can turn the wheel, rock the wheel back & forth slightly to hear for clunks in the steering gear.<BR>- brakes, brakes & brakes<BR>- check eng light should come on with the key on but engine not running.<BR>- bring a phillips screw driver and piece of wire about 3 or 4 inches long and 1/4 inch stripped at both ends. Take the trim off around the cig lighter on center console. the diag connector is on the right side.  With the key off insert the wire into the two right most (passenger side) holes on the bottom row.  Turn the key on and watch the check engine light.  should blink 1,2 (or code 12) three times then repeat.  If it doesn't walk away.<P>that should do it. GL!<P>Oh, if it has 16" or larger wheels, look for cracks in the windshield and pillars... (JK private PFF joke. You can look it up with search)<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 07-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||stsmithgt||07-27-2001||09:59 AM||||Oh yeah. Nice addition.  I couldn't think of those off the top of my head.  My steering used to clunk.  One of these days I'll get a cooler thermostat, other than the stock one. <BR>-I'd say it's hard to tell tread wear because they probably put different tires on from when they got it, but it doesn't hurt to look.  If you looked at mine, you could tell I've been scrubbing some corners pretty hard. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>-When you start it for the first time.(if it starts) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">, listen to it the instant it fires to hear how loose the motor is.  Hopefully it's the first time it was started that day to get a more acurate result. GOOD LUCK||||reg||6
Z||000015||mrgone||07-27-2001||04:28 PM||||1400 a year for insurance? hahaha<BR>thats nothing.<BR>im 19 and i have a clean record and a driving course and my insurance (LIABILITY ONLY, NO THEFT OR FIRE) is 3000$ a year for a 1986 fiero 2m4(2000$ US)<P>so 1400 for a V8 in a car actually worth somthing||||reg||1
